{{!-- ENCOUNTERS — what has been gathered for one session or one scene, in named bundles: the
      monsters, the read-aloud page, the battle map, the treasure table, the arcanum somebody is
      about to find. The one tab on this sheet that holds POINTERS to other documents rather than
      prose about them.

      TWO LISTS, ONE CARD. What is still to come is at the top; what has been run is filed into
      the Completed fold at the foot, and the tick on a card is what sends it there. Both lists
      print `gm-encounter-card.hbs`, which is where everything about a single encounter lives —
      including the one button that reads differently in the two places. The split itself is made
      in JS off each encounter's `used` flag (gm-encounters-tab.js), never off the DOM.

      NOTHING IS LOST BY COMPLETING ONE. A filed encounter keeps its notes, its collected rows,
      its Deploy button and its dropzone, and one press of the arrow puts it back on the live
      list. That is the whole reason the trash is a separate button behind a pencil: the frequent
      act is reversible and one click, and the final one asks first.

      WRITES DO NOT GO THROUGH THE FORM. Every field here is handled by gm-encounters-tab.js and
      saved straight to `actor.system.encounters`, so none of them carries a `name` attribute: a
      `name` on an input inside this sheet's <form> puts it in core's submit data, where the array
      indices would expand into an OBJECT keyed "0", "1", ... and quietly replace the list with it.

      ONE PENCIL for the whole tab, in the panel's corner, and it guards exactly the two delete
      buttons — in BOTH lists, because the `--reading` class sits on the <section> that wraps
      them. Everything else stays live while it is off, which is the point rather than an
      oversight: the reason this tab exists is to be dropped into and noted on mid-session, and a
      tab that has to be unlocked first is one nobody uses. What the pencil is FOR is that "delete
      this encounter" is one press on the head of a shut row, and now the ONLY button on that head:
      a lone 22px square, at the end of a row a GM drags things onto and types in constantly, whose
      one press throws a session's prep away.

      NOT `stonetop-readonly`. That class is the sheet-wide lock (pointer-events off every input,
      textarea and button, then an allow-list), and on this tab it would put out exactly the fields
      the paragraph above says have to stay lit. `--reading` hides the two buttons it is about and
      touches nothing else.

      `data-tab="encounters"` earns the rail's stack glyph; the icon table in stonetop.css is one
      flat list keyed by data-tab with no per-sheet scope. --}}
<div class="tab encounters steading-edit-section" data-group="primary" data-tab="encounters">
	{{> "stonetop.steading-section-toggle" section="encounters" editing=stonetop.edit.encounters}}
	<section class="sheet-tab stonetop-gm-encounters{{#unless stonetop.edit.encounters}} stonetop-gm-encounters--reading{{/unless}}">

		<p class="steading-threats-intro">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.intro"}}</p>

		{{!-- THE LIVE LIST CARRIES NO HEADING OF ITS OWN. It is what the tab IS, sitting directly
		      under the tab's own intro, and a "Prepared" heading over it would only be there to
		      make a pair with the fold below — which is rendered at all only once something has
		      been run. A heading that exists to balance a section that is usually absent is a
		      heading that reads as a section boundary on an empty tab. --}}
		{{#if stonetop.encounters.active.length}}
		<ul class="stonetop-gm-encounter-list">
			{{#each stonetop.encounters.active}}
			{{> "stonetop.gm-encounter-card"}}
			{{/each}}
		</ul>
		{{else}}
		{{!-- Two empty states, because "nothing here yet" and "you have run everything you
		      prepared" are different facts and the second one is wrong advice: there is no need
		      to tell a GM to drag something in when what they most likely want is to lift one
		      back out of the fold two inches below. --}}
		<p class="stonetop-gm-encounter-empty">
			{{#if stonetop.encounters.completed.length}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.emptyAllDone"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.empty"}}{{/if}}
		</p>
		{{/if}}

		{{!-- The add bar stays with the LIVE list, above the fold rather than at the true foot of
		      the tab: what it adds is a new encounter, which lands on that list, and a bar sitting
		      under a season's worth of completed ones would be a bar a GM has to scroll past a
		      history to reach. --}}
		{{> "stonetop.gm-prep-add-bar" kind="stonetop-gm-encounter"
		    label=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.add")}}

		{{!-- COMPLETED, and rendered only when there is something in it, so an untouched tab shows
		      one list and not two.

		      It folds, and starts OPEN like every other section on this sheet: a section shut on
		      first open is one a GM never learns is there. Folding it is a choice the caret offers
		      and the sheet then remembers per user — which is exactly what a GM three sessions in
		      will want, and exactly what a GM one session in must not have chosen for them.

		      The caret rides ON THE HEADING (via `section-heading`) rather than in a corner,
		      because this section has no pencil of its own: the tab's single pencil in the panel's
		      top-right corner already reaches the delete buttons inside here, and a second one
		      would be two controls for one lock. A lone caret floating in an otherwise empty
		      corner is what the wonder tab's guidance section avoids the same way.

		      The heading's FOLLOWING SIBLINGS are what the fold hides (`_sectionFoldTargets`), so
		      the box around this pair is load-bearing: without it the walk would run on past the
		      list and out of the tab. Nothing else may go in this box, and a SECOND heading in it
		      would stop the caret claiming at the first. --}}
		{{#if stonetop.encounters.completed.length}}
		<div class="stonetop-gm-encounters-completed">
			{{> "stonetop.section-heading" title=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.completed")
			    note=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.completedNote")
			    collapse="gm-encounters-completed"}}
			<ul class="stonetop-gm-encounter-list stonetop-gm-encounter-list--completed">
				{{#each stonetop.encounters.completed}}
				{{> "stonetop.gm-encounter-card"}}
				{{/each}}
			</ul>
		</div>
		{{/if}}

	</section>
</div>
